{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices across most horizons (often by ~1–4 EUR, occasionally more). Apply an upward level shift to the entire path, with a larger shift when the recent trend is up.",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: after an upswing begins, the model increases too slowly and misses the magnitude of the rally. Add a momentum/trend term based on the last 5–10 day slope (steepen the forecast path when recent returns are positive).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: underprediction grows with horizon (errors tend to widen in weeks 2–4). Use a bias that increases with lead time (e.g., small uplift days 1–5, larger uplift days 10–30).",
    "Excess mean reversion: the model pulls forecasts back toward mid-60s too aggressively, while outcomes stay elevated near upper-60s/around 70. Reduce mean-reversion strength when spot is above its 20–30d average and the average is rising.",
    "Late-horizon downside overshoot: in some runs the model projects sharp declines late in the window (e.g., drops into low-60s) that do not materialize. Impose a floor or dampen negative drift beyond ~day 20 unless recent realized prices show sustained declines."
  ]
}
